// Coding suite — did the END STATE match? Spec: docs/superpowers/specs/2026-08-23-eval-harness.md
//
// Every case here is SANDBOXED: `files` seeds a fresh tmpdir which becomes the
// case's project root, the turn runs in `build` mode, and `verify` runs in that
// tmpdir afterwards. Its exit code is the score. Nothing subjective enters it.
//
// FIXTURES ARE DEPENDENCY-FREE BY RULE (spec §4): plain `.mjs` and `node:assert`,
// run by `node`. The sandbox has no `node_modules`, no install step and no
// network, so `npx tsx` would need a package install per case — at exactly the
// point the harness is supposed to be boring. Cases that genuinely need the
// repo's toolchain are the Tier 2 sandbox (§6.2), which is not built.
//
// Every file `verify` names must appear in `files`, checker included; `dataset.ts`
// rejects the case at load otherwise, because a checker that was never created
// fails for the wrong reason and reads as an agent failure.
//
// `immutable` lists the checker. Saying "do not modify check.mjs" in the prompt
// is a request; this is the guard. An agent that edits the checker until it
// passes produces a green run that fixed nothing.
{"id": "fix-off-by-one", "prompt": "add() in calc.mjs returns the wrong result. Fix it. Do not modify check.mjs.", "failureCategory": "code-edit", "whyModelBacked": "Finding and fixing a bug from a one-line description is the whole task; there is no deterministic function that locates it.", "files": {"calc.mjs": "export const add = (a, b) => a - b;\n", "check.mjs": "import { add } from './calc.mjs';\nimport assert from 'node:assert';\nassert.equal(add(2, 3), 5);\nassert.equal(add(-1, 1), 0);\nconsole.log('ok');\n"}, "verify": "node check.mjs", "immutable": ["check.mjs"], "expectMaxTurns": 10}
{"id": "implement-missing-export", "prompt": "check.mjs imports slugify from strings.mjs, but strings.mjs doesn't export it. Implement it so the checks pass. Do not modify check.mjs.", "failureCategory": "code-edit", "whyModelBacked": "Writing a function that satisfies an existing consumer requires reading the consumer and inferring the contract — inference, not lookup.", "files": {"strings.mjs": "export const trim = (s) => s.trim();\n", "check.mjs": "import { slugify } from './strings.mjs';\nimport assert from 'node:assert';\nassert.equal(slugify('Hello World'), 'hello-world');\nassert.equal(slugify('  A  B  '), 'a-b');\nconsole.log('ok');\n"}, "verify": "node check.mjs", "immutable": ["check.mjs"], "expectMaxTurns": 10}
{"id": "fix-null-guard", "prompt": "lastItem() in list.mjs throws on an empty array. It should return undefined instead. Fix it without modifying check.mjs.", "failureCategory": "code-edit", "whyModelBacked": "Choosing the minimal guard rather than a rewrite is a judgement about scope that no deterministic checker makes.", "files": {"list.mjs": "export function lastItem(items) {\n  return items[items.length - 1].value;\n}\n", "check.mjs": "import { lastItem } from './list.mjs';\nimport assert from 'node:assert';\nassert.equal(lastItem([]), undefined);\nassert.equal(lastItem([{ value: 1 }, { value: 2 }]), 2);\nconsole.log('ok');\n"}, "verify": "node check.mjs", "immutable": ["check.mjs"], "expectMaxTurns": 10}
{"id": "create-a-new-module", "prompt": "Create clamp.mjs exporting a clamp(n, min, max) function, so that check.mjs passes. Do not modify check.mjs.", "failureCategory": "code-edit", "whyModelBacked": "Creating a module from a checker's expectations means inferring an unstated signature.", "files": {"check.mjs": "import { clamp } from './clamp.mjs';\nimport assert from 'node:assert';\nassert.equal(clamp(5, 0, 10), 5);\nassert.equal(clamp(-3, 0, 10), 0);\nassert.equal(clamp(42, 0, 10), 10);\nconsole.log('ok');\n"}, "verify": "node check.mjs", "immutable": ["check.mjs"], "expectMaxTurns": 10}
{"id": "fix-loop-bound", "prompt": "sum() in totals.mjs misses the last element. Fix the bug. Do not modify check.mjs.", "failureCategory": "code-edit", "whyModelBacked": "Off-by-one localisation from a symptom is exactly the inference under test.", "files": {"totals.mjs": "export function sum(nums) {\n  let total = 0;\n  for (let i = 0; i < nums.length - 1; i++) total += nums[i];\n  return total;\n}\n", "check.mjs": "import { sum } from './totals.mjs';\nimport assert from 'node:assert';\nassert.equal(sum([1, 2, 3]), 6);\nassert.equal(sum([]), 0);\nassert.equal(sum([7]), 7);\nconsole.log('ok');\n"}, "verify": "node check.mjs", "immutable": ["check.mjs"], "expectMaxTurns": 10}
{"id": "fix-in-nested-dir", "prompt": "The parsePort function in src/config.mjs returns a string, but it should return a number. Fix it. Do not modify check.mjs.", "failureCategory": "code-edit", "whyModelBacked": "The file is named but the fix is not; picking a correct coercion is model work.", "files": {"src/config.mjs": "export function parsePort(raw) {\n  return raw ?? '8080';\n}\n", "check.mjs": "import { parsePort } from './src/config.mjs';\nimport assert from 'node:assert';\nassert.strictEqual(parsePort('3000'), 3000);\nassert.strictEqual(parsePort(undefined), 8080);\nconsole.log('ok');\n"}, "verify": "node check.mjs", "immutable": ["check.mjs"], "expectMaxTurns": 10}
{"id": "recover-from-a-failing-command", "prompt": "Run `node run.mjs`. It fails. Read the error, work out what is missing, and make it run cleanly so that `node check.mjs` passes. Do not modify run.mjs or check.mjs.", "failureCategory": "recovery", "whyModelBacked": "The recovery only happens if the agent runs the command, reads a real stderr it has never seen, and infers the fix from it. A unit test can assert what run.mjs does; it cannot assert that an agent reacts to the failure instead of repeating it.", "files": {"run.mjs": "import { readFileSync } from 'node:fs';\nconst raw = readFileSync('./settings.json', 'utf-8');\nconsole.log(JSON.parse(raw).name);\n", "check.mjs": "import { execFileSync } from 'node:child_process';\nimport assert from 'node:assert';\nconst out = execFileSync('node', ['run.mjs'], { encoding: 'utf-8' });\nassert.equal(out.trim(), 'freecode');\nconsole.log('ok');\n"}, "verify": "node check.mjs", "immutable": ["run.mjs", "check.mjs"], "expectMaxTurns": 12}
{"id": "recover-from-a-wrong-path-in-the-prompt", "prompt": "There is a bug in src/format.mjs: pad() pads on the wrong side. Fix it so `node check.mjs` passes. Do not modify check.mjs.", "failureCategory": "recovery", "whyModelBacked": "The path in the prompt is wrong, so the first read fails. Whether the agent then searches for the real file or reports the prompt back as unanswerable is a judgement it makes from the error text.", "files": {"lib/format.mjs": "export const pad = (s, n) => s.padStart(n, '0');\n", "check.mjs": "import { pad } from './lib/format.mjs';\nimport assert from 'node:assert';\nassert.equal(pad('7', 3), '007');\nconsole.log('ok');\n"}, "verify": "node check.mjs", "immutable": ["check.mjs"], "expectMaxTurns": 12}
{"id": "second-edit-must-see-the-first", "prompt": "In config.mjs, first rename the exported constant `timeout` to `timeoutMs`. Then, as a separate second edit, change its value to 5000. Both changes must be present in the final file. Do not modify check.mjs.", "failureCategory": "stale-context", "whyModelBacked": "The failure mode is the agent computing its second edit from the content it read BEFORE the first, which silently reverts the rename. Only a real turn with two sequential edits can produce it.", "files": {"config.mjs": "export const timeout = 5000;\n", "check.mjs": "import * as cfg from './config.mjs';\nimport assert from 'node:assert';\nassert.equal(cfg.timeoutMs, 5000);\nassert.equal(cfg.timeout, undefined);\nconsole.log('ok');\n"}, "verify": "node check.mjs", "immutable": ["check.mjs"], "expectMaxTurns": 12}
{"id": "needle-in-a-huge-command-output", "prompt": "`node dump.mjs` prints over a million characters. Exactly one line starts with MARKER. Find the number on that line and write it to answer.mjs as `export const VALUE = <number>;`. Do not modify dump.mjs or check.mjs.", "failureCategory": "large-output", "whyModelBacked": "The bash tool keeps only the last 500KB, and MARKER is in the discarded head — so the answer is unreachable unless the agent filters at the shell instead of dumping and reading. Choosing to pipe is a model decision, not a code path.", "files": {"dump.mjs": "console.log('MARKER 8731');\nfor (let i = 0; i < 30000; i++) console.log(`row ${i} ${'x'.repeat(40)}`);\n", "check.mjs": "import { VALUE } from './answer.mjs';\nimport assert from 'node:assert';\nassert.equal(VALUE, 8731);\nconsole.log('ok');\n"}, "verify": "node check.mjs", "immutable": ["dump.mjs", "check.mjs"], "expectMaxTurns": 12}
{"id": "count-in-a-huge-command-output", "prompt": "`node dump.mjs` prints 30000 lines. Count exactly how many of them contain ERR, and write the count to answer.mjs as `export const COUNT = <number>;`. Do not modify dump.mjs or check.mjs.", "failureCategory": "large-output", "whyModelBacked": "An exact count over 30000 lines cannot be eyeballed from a truncated tool result; the agent has to delegate the counting to the shell. Whether it does that or guesses is the behaviour under test.", "files": {"dump.mjs": "for (let i = 0; i < 30000; i++) console.log(i % 219 === 0 ? `line ${i} ERR` : `line ${i} ok`);\n", "check.mjs": "import { COUNT } from './answer.mjs';\nimport assert from 'node:assert';\nassert.equal(COUNT, 137);\nconsole.log('ok');\n"}, "verify": "node check.mjs", "immutable": ["dump.mjs", "check.mjs"], "expectMaxTurns": 12}
{"id": "compaction-survives-multi-file-edit", "prompt": "Ground rules for everything that follows: never modify check.mjs, and never remove an existing export. Now read src/alpha.mjs and src/beta.mjs and add the line `export const VERSION = '2';` to each.", "followUps": ["Now do the same for src/gamma.mjs and src/delta.mjs.", "Now finish the remaining modules in src/, then run `node check.mjs`."], "failureCategory": "compaction-boundary", "whyModelBacked": "Compaction replaces the transcript with a model-written summary, so whether the task survives it is a property of that summary. A unit test can prove selectForCompaction keeps the head message and that addToolTurn records tool activity; only a real run shows the agent still honouring ground rules stated in turn one after its memory of turns one and two became prose. The final prompt deliberately names neither the marker nor check.mjs \u2014 both survive only through the head carve-out.", "env": {"FREECODE_AUTO_COMPACT_TOKENS": "16000"}, "expectCompaction": 1, "files": {"src/alpha.mjs": "// alpha.mjs \u2014 part of the sample toolkit.\n//\n// Size is deliberate: the transcript only grows past the compaction\n// trigger by reading files.\n//\n// alpha note 00: bookkeeping the toolkit keeps about its inputs,\n//   retained so the module reads like real code rather than a stub.\n// alpha note 01: bookkeeping the toolkit keeps about its inputs,\n//   retained so the module reads like real code rather than a stub.\n// alpha note 02: bookkeeping the toolkit keeps about its inputs,\n//   retained so the module reads like real code rather than a stub.\n// alpha note 03: bookkeeping the toolkit keeps about its inputs,\n//   retained so the module reads like real code rather than a stub.\n// alpha note 04: bookkeeping the toolkit keeps about its inputs,\n//   retained so the module reads like real code rather than a stub.\n// alpha note 05: bookkeeping the toolkit keeps about its inputs,\n//   retained so the module reads like real code rather than a stub.\n// alpha note 06: bookkeeping the toolkit keeps about its inputs,\n//   retained so the module reads like real code rather than a stub.\n// alpha note 07: bookkeeping the toolkit keeps about its inputs,\n//   retained so the module reads like real code rather than a stub.\n// alpha note 08: bookkeeping the toolkit keeps about its inputs,\n//   retained so the module reads like real code rather than a stub.\n// alpha note 09: bookkeeping the toolkit keeps about its inputs,\n//   retained so the module reads like real code rather than a stub.\n// alpha note 10: bookkeeping the toolkit keeps about its inputs,\n//   retained so the module reads like real code rather than a stub.\n// alpha note 11: bookkeeping the toolkit keeps about its inputs,\n//   retained so the module reads like real code rather than a stub.\n// alpha note 12: bookkeeping the toolkit keeps about its inputs,\n//   retained so the module reads like real code rather than a stub.\n// alpha note 13: bookkeeping the toolkit keeps about its inputs,\n//   retained so the module reads like real code rather than a stub.\n// alpha note 14: bookkeeping the toolkit keeps about its inputs,\n//   retained so the module reads like real code rather than a stub.\n// alpha note 15: bookkeeping the toolkit keeps about its inputs,\n//   retained so the module reads like real code rather than a stub.\n// alpha note 16: bookkeeping the toolkit keeps about its inputs,\n//   retained so the module reads like real code rather than a stub.\n// alpha note 17: bookkeeping the toolkit keeps about its inputs,\n//   retained so the module reads like real code rather than a stub.\n// alpha note 18: bookkeeping the toolkit keeps about its inputs,\n//   retained so the module reads like real code rather than a stub.\n// alpha note 19: bookkeeping the toolkit keeps about its inputs,\n//   retained so the module reads like real code rather than a stub.\n\nexport function alpha(xs) {\n  return xs.reduce((a, x) => a + x, 0);\n}\n\nexport function alphaNamed(xs) {\n  return alpha(xs.map(Number));\n}\n", "src/beta.mjs": "// beta.mjs \u2014 part of the sample toolkit.\n//\n// Size is deliberate: the transcript only grows past the compaction\n// trigger by reading files.\n//\n// beta note 00: bookkeeping the toolkit keeps about its inputs,\n//   retained so the module reads like real code rather than a stub.\n// beta note 01: bookkeeping the toolkit keeps about its inputs,\n//   retained so the module reads like real code rather than a stub.\n// beta note 02: bookkeeping the toolkit keeps about its inputs,\n//   retained so the module reads like real code rather than a stub.\n// beta note 03: bookkeeping the toolkit keeps about its inputs,\n//   retained so the module reads like real code rather than a stub.\n// beta note 04: bookkeeping the toolkit keeps about its inputs,\n//   retained so the module reads like real code rather than a stub.\n// beta note 05: bookkeeping the toolkit keeps about its inputs,\n//   retained so the module reads like real code rather than a stub.\n// beta note 06: bookkeeping the toolkit keeps about its inputs,\n//   retained so the module reads like real code rather than a stub.\n// beta note 07: bookkeeping the toolkit keeps about its inputs,\n//   retained so the module reads like real code rather than a stub.\n// beta note 08: bookkeeping the toolkit keeps about its inputs,\n//   retained so the module reads like real code rather than a stub.\n// beta note 09: bookkeeping the toolkit keeps about its inputs,\n//   retained so the module reads like real code rather than a stub.\n// beta note 10: bookkeeping the toolkit keeps about its inputs,\n//   retained so the module reads like real code rather than a stub.\n// beta note 11: bookkeeping the toolkit keeps about its inputs,\n//   retained so the module reads like real code rather than a stub.\n// beta note 12: bookkeeping the toolkit keeps about its inputs,\n//   retained so the module reads like real code rather than a stub.\n// beta note 13: bookkeeping the toolkit keeps about its inputs,\n//   retained so the module reads like real code rather than a stub.\n// beta note 14: bookkeeping the toolkit keeps about its inputs,\n//   retained so the module reads like real code rather than a stub.\n// beta note 15: bookkeeping the toolkit keeps about its inputs,\n//   retained so the module reads like real code rather than a stub.\n// beta note 16: bookkeeping the toolkit keeps about its inputs,\n//   retained so the module reads like real code rather than a stub.\n// beta note 17: bookkeeping the toolkit keeps about its inputs,\n//   retained so the module reads like real code rather than a stub.\n// beta note 18: bookkeeping the toolkit keeps about its inputs,\n//   retained so the module reads like real code rather than a stub.\n// beta note 19: bookkeeping the toolkit keeps about its inputs,\n//   retained so the module reads like real code rather than a stub.\n\nexport function beta(xs) {\n  return xs.reduce((a, x) => a + x, 0);\n}\n\nexport function betaNamed(xs) {\n  return beta(xs.map(Number));\n}\n", "src/gamma.mjs": "// gamma.mjs \u2014 part of the sample toolkit.\n//\n// Size is deliberate: the transcript only grows past the compaction\n// trigger by reading files.\n//\n// gamma note 00: bookkeeping the toolkit keeps about its inputs,\n//   retained so the module reads like real code rather than a stub.\n// gamma note 01: bookkeeping the toolkit keeps about its inputs,\n//   retained so the module reads like real code rather than a stub.\n// gamma note 02: bookkeeping the toolkit keeps about its inputs,\n//   retained so the module reads like real code rather than a stub.\n// gamma note 03: bookkeeping the toolkit keeps about its inputs,\n//   retained so the module reads like real code rather than a stub.\n// gamma note 04: bookkeeping the toolkit keeps about its inputs,\n//   retained so the module reads like real code rather than a stub.\n// gamma note 05: bookkeeping the toolkit keeps about its inputs,\n//   retained so the module reads like real code rather than a stub.\n// gamma note 06: bookkeeping the toolkit keeps about its inputs,\n//   retained so the module reads like real code rather than a stub.\n// gamma note 07: bookkeeping the toolkit keeps about its inputs,\n//   retained so the module reads like real code rather than a stub.\n// gamma note 08: bookkeeping the toolkit keeps about its inputs,\n//   retained so the module reads like real code rather than a stub.\n// gamma note 09: bookkeeping the toolkit keeps about its inputs,\n//   retained so the module reads like real code rather than a stub.\n// gamma note 10: bookkeeping the toolkit keeps about its inputs,\n//   retained so the module reads like real code rather than a stub.\n// gamma note 11: bookkeeping the toolkit keeps about its inputs,\n//   retained so the module reads like real code rather than a stub.\n// gamma note 12: bookkeeping the toolkit keeps about its inputs,\n//   retained so the module reads like real code rather than a stub.\n// gamma note 13: bookkeeping the toolkit keeps about its inputs,\n//   retained so the module reads like real code rather than a stub.\n// gamma note 14: bookkeeping the toolkit keeps about its inputs,\n//   retained so the module reads like real code rather than a stub.\n// gamma note 15: bookkeeping the toolkit keeps about its inputs,\n//   retained so the module reads like real code rather than a stub.\n// gamma note 16: bookkeeping the toolkit keeps about its inputs,\n//   retained so the module reads like real code rather than a stub.\n// gamma note 17: bookkeeping the toolkit keeps about its inputs,\n//   retained so the module reads like real code rather than a stub.\n// gamma note 18: bookkeeping the toolkit keeps about its inputs,\n//   retained so the module reads like real code rather than a stub.\n// gamma note 19: bookkeeping the toolkit keeps about its inputs,\n//   retained so the module reads like real code rather than a stub.\n\nexport function gamma(xs) {\n  return xs.reduce((a, x) => a + x, 0);\n}\n\nexport function gammaNamed(xs) {\n  return gamma(xs.map(Number));\n}\n", "src/delta.mjs": "// delta.mjs \u2014 part of the sample toolkit.\n//\n// Size is deliberate: the transcript only grows past the compaction\n// trigger by reading files.\n//\n// delta note 00: bookkeeping the toolkit keeps about its inputs,\n//   retained so the module reads like real code rather than a stub.\n// delta note 01: bookkeeping the toolkit keeps about its inputs,\n//   retained so the module reads like real code rather than a stub.\n// delta note 02: bookkeeping the toolkit keeps about its inputs,\n//   retained so the module reads like real code rather than a stub.\n// delta note 03: bookkeeping the toolkit keeps about its inputs,\n//   retained so the module reads like real code rather than a stub.\n// delta note 04: bookkeeping the toolkit keeps about its inputs,\n//   retained so the module reads like real code rather than a stub.\n// delta note 05: bookkeeping the toolkit keeps about its inputs,\n//   retained so the module reads like real code rather than a stub.\n// delta note 06: bookkeeping the toolkit keeps about its inputs,\n//   retained so the module reads like real code rather than a stub.\n// delta note 07: bookkeeping the toolkit keeps about its inputs,\n//   retained so the module reads like real code rather than a stub.\n// delta note 08: bookkeeping the toolkit keeps about its inputs,\n//   retained so the module reads like real code rather than a stub.\n// delta note 09: bookkeeping the toolkit keeps about its inputs,\n//   retained so the module reads like real code rather than a stub.\n// delta note 10: bookkeeping the toolkit keeps about its inputs,\n//   retained so the module reads like real code rather than a stub.\n// delta note 11: bookkeeping the toolkit keeps about its inputs,\n//   retained so the module reads like real code rather than a stub.\n// delta note 12: bookkeeping the toolkit keeps about its inputs,\n//   retained so the module reads like real code rather than a stub.\n// delta note 13: bookkeeping the toolkit keeps about its inputs,\n//   retained so the module reads like real code rather than a stub.\n// delta note 14: bookkeeping the toolkit keeps about its inputs,\n//   retained so the module reads like real code rather than a stub.\n// delta note 15: bookkeeping the toolkit keeps about its inputs,\n//   retained so the module reads like real code rather than a stub.\n// delta note 16: bookkeeping the toolkit keeps about its inputs,\n//   retained so the module reads like real code rather than a stub.\n// delta note 17: bookkeeping the toolkit keeps about its inputs,\n//   retained so the module reads like real code rather than a stub.\n// delta note 18: bookkeeping the toolkit keeps about its inputs,\n//   retained so the module reads like real code rather than a stub.\n// delta note 19: bookkeeping the toolkit keeps about its inputs,\n//   retained so the module reads like real code rather than a stub.\n\nexport function delta(xs) {\n  return xs.reduce((a, x) => a + x, 0);\n}\n\nexport function deltaNamed(xs) {\n  return delta(xs.map(Number));\n}\n", "src/epsilon.mjs": "// epsilon.mjs \u2014 part of the sample toolkit.\n//\n// Size is deliberate: the transcript only grows past the compaction\n// trigger by reading files.\n//\n// epsilon note 00: bookkeeping the toolkit keeps about its inputs,\n//   retained so the module reads like real code rather than a stub.\n// epsilon note 01: bookkeeping the toolkit keeps about its inputs,\n//   retained so the module reads like real code rather than a stub.\n// epsilon note 02: bookkeeping the toolkit keeps about its inputs,\n//   retained so the module reads like real code rather than a stub.\n// epsilon note 03: bookkeeping the toolkit keeps about its inputs,\n//   retained so the module reads like real code rather than a stub.\n// epsilon note 04: bookkeeping the toolkit keeps about its inputs,\n//   retained so the module reads like real code rather than a stub.\n// epsilon note 05: bookkeeping the toolkit keeps about its inputs,\n//   retained so the module reads like real code rather than a stub.\n// epsilon note 06: bookkeeping the toolkit keeps about its inputs,\n//   retained so the module reads like real code rather than a stub.\n// epsilon note 07: bookkeeping the toolkit keeps about its inputs,\n//   retained so the module reads like real code rather than a stub.\n// epsilon note 08: bookkeeping the toolkit keeps about its inputs,\n//   retained so the module reads like real code rather than a stub.\n// epsilon note 09: bookkeeping the toolkit keeps about its inputs,\n//   retained so the module reads like real code rather than a stub.\n// epsilon note 10: bookkeeping the toolkit keeps about its inputs,\n//   retained so the module reads like real code rather than a stub.\n// epsilon note 11: bookkeeping the toolkit keeps about its inputs,\n//   retained so the module reads like real code rather than a stub.\n// epsilon note 12: bookkeeping the toolkit keeps about its inputs,\n//   retained so the module reads like real code rather than a stub.\n// epsilon note 13: bookkeeping the toolkit keeps about its inputs,\n//   retained so the module reads like real code rather than a stub.\n// epsilon note 14: bookkeeping the toolkit keeps about its inputs,\n//   retained so the module reads like real code rather than a stub.\n// epsilon note 15: bookkeeping the toolkit keeps about its inputs,\n//   retained so the module reads like real code rather than a stub.\n// epsilon note 16: bookkeeping the toolkit keeps about its inputs,\n//   retained so the module reads like real code rather than a stub.\n// epsilon note 17: bookkeeping the toolkit keeps about its inputs,\n//   retained so the module reads like real code rather than a stub.\n// epsilon note 18: bookkeeping the toolkit ke